      <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>Overall, your house average for air leakage. There are some key areas where air leakage occurs. Air leakage was observed: 

I recommend to outfit electrical outlets located along the exterior walls with an electrical wall plate gasket. 
I recommend replacing the weather stripping along all of your exterior doors. Using a screw in aluminum style weather stripping may help to create a better seal, as well as using an adjustable strike plate to ensure the door makes good contact with the weather stripping while closed.  
Air leakage was also observed throughf the bathroom exhaust fans. I recommend you replace your exhaust fans with an ENERGY STARÂ® rated model, which will have a new damper built into the new unit.
The attic access hatch had quite a bit of air leakage entering from the attic space. I recommend using a removable caulking to seal up the access hatch. 
There was air leekage observed coming from the attic space in the backroom (the back entrance). Air leakage was observed between the ceiling joists (where the drywall was removed), as well as around the light fixture. 

Savings: I modeled a 10% reduction in air leakage, that is estimated to save $100 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 3.3 GJ, and qualify for $ 500 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). Achieving a minimum 20% reduction qualifies for $750, and a minimum 30% reduction qualifies for $1,000.

Unless you are participating in the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), that requires a minimum additional R10 insulation be added to the above foundation, main floor exterior walls, there are currently no rebates for air leakage reduction.

NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant between $715 and $1,300 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on the level achieved. This is one of the most cost-effective energy-saving measures you can undertake. 
An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional. 
</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>The above foundation exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 with R20 batt insulation. As per your plans we duscussed, I have modelled adding 3.0 inches of Neopor (R14.1) to 71.4% (excluding the front brick wall). 
Under the new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program that was launched on April 1, 2019, there are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+).

Savings:
By increasing 71.4% of the exterior walls by R14, you could potentially save $320 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 9.8 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 2,142 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP).

NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of up to $5,000 is available for adding insulation to exterior wall area of your home. Insulation grants are based on the percentage of wall area to which the insulation has been added and the amount of insulation added. A minimum of 20% of the exterior wall area of your home, excluding foundation walls, must be insulated in order to qualify for a grant.
</mainWalls>
                <foundation>Your basement pony walls appear to be 2x6 framing with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation. In conjuction with the above main wall insulation plans, I have modelled 3.0 inches (R14.1) Neopor insulation to the pony walls. 

Savings: By adding 3 inches (R14.1) Neopor insulation to the pony walls, you could potentially save $130 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 4.1 GJ, and qualify for up to a $400 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) if you complete an eligible above foundation wall insulation upgrade.
NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.

If you do not to complete a DHERP eligible above foundation wall insulation upgrade, you would still be eligible for up to a $200 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.
For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative offers grants of up to $1,950 for adding insulation to basement walls. Insulating basement walls can reduce energy costs and improve comfort. Grants are based on the RSI/R-value of the added insulation and the percentage of wall area insulated. 9 
The current amount of insulation does not affect the grant amounts. A minimum of 20% of the exterior basement wall area must be insulated.
</foundation>
                <windows>The windows in this house are triple pane vinyl windows, that are, for the most part, in ok shape. I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all of these windows (10 windows total) with ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.21 BTU per hour.

Savings: By replacing these older windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $80 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 2.5 GJ, and qualify for up to $2,500 in rebates (10 existing windows @ $250 each) through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) if you complete an eligible above foundation wall insulation upgrade.

Unless you are participating in the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), that requires a minimum additional R10 insulation be added to the above foundation, main floor exterior walls, there are currently no rebates for window upgrades.
NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, grants of either $162.50 or $325 per window rough opening are available when an ENERGY STAR certified or ENERGY STAR certified Most Efficient window is installed to replace an existing window. Replacing old, damaged or leaky windows with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy, improve comfort and reduce noise. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.
</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>As your boiler is currently indirectly heating your domextic hot water, this upgrade has been included with the recommended boiler upgrade. </HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>Your house is currently heated with an 85% efficient oil boiler. In conjunction with a Deep Home Energy Retrofit of your house (adding more insulation and reducing the air leakage) I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 96% AFUE. As your current boiler is indirectly heating your domestic hot water, the savings below also reflect the savings if the new boiler continuing to do so. 

Savings: By replacing your current oil boiler with a 96% AFUE condensing propane boiler, you could potentially save $1,600 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 37.0 GJ, and qualify for up to $3,500 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). While updating your boiler, you should also install ECM circulation pumps and qualify for 50% up to $500 in rebates per pump (up to 5 per fiscal year) from the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP). 
NOTE: Please see the "Additional Comments" section of this report for important information about the DHERP, and your eligibility to participate. 
If you do not to complete a DHERP eligible wall insulation upgrade, you would still be eligible for up to $1,750 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.
For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Under the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $1,600 is available for replacing an oil- or gas-fired furnace or boiler with an ENERGY STAR certified natural gas- or propane-fired furnace or boiler. To determine eligibility, confirm that the unit is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.
</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program

To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total above foundation, exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits, and excludes any new additions. To be eligible for a rebate through the DHERP, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the Arctic Energy Alliance.

DHERP rebates are provided on a first-come, first-serve basis for as long as funding remains available. Receiving a pre-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation does not in itself guarantee the availability of funding. Homeowners who have demonstrated their intention to proceed with an eligible above foundation, exterior wall insulation upgrade will be:
      A. Provided with a conditional "Pre-Authorization" of DHERP funding, if funding is still available; or
      B. Placed on a DHERP funding waiting list, should funding become available

For more information and program guidelines, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

As you research if you will indeed participate in the DHERP, please note the upgrade recommendations outlined within this report are estimated to qualify for up to $10,042 in rebates. The following are the estimated rebates per component of the DHERP:

     - $2,142 for the wall insulation
     - $2,500 for the windows
     - $400    for the foundation insulation
     - $3,500 for the heating system
     - $500    for the air leakage reduction
     - $1,000 for the bonus rebate (27.2% improvement by completing all of the recommendations listed). 
*As the boiler has the largest potential, the improvement would be 10.6% without this recommendation. 

NOTE: If you do not proceed with an eligible main floor wall insulation upgrade, than you are not eligible for the DHERP - and the only rebates from the list above that would still apply would be the attic &amp; foundation insulation and the heating system, however these would only be 50% of what is listed above.

When products purchased outside of the NWT, in which are related to the above incentives, are eligible for 50% of the amount.  
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

LED Lights Â Hydro Community

Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.
Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 
To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 
For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

FUEL COSTS
The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:
Electricity - $18.13 min, $0.32 per kWh
Oil  - $1.23 per litre 
Propane - $0.75 per litre
Wood - $405 per cord 
Pellets - $5.72 per bag
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

DISCLAIMER
The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

YOUR ENERGY ADVISOR
Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.

Sincerely,

Michael Goodwin
Senior Energy Management Specialist
Arctic Energy Alliance

Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!
</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
